; hark: root-hints = 127.0.10.1
; hark: dnssec-zone = .

SCENARIO_BEGIN NSEC next_domain keeps its case (RFC 6840 §5.1)

; RFC 6840 §5.1, verbatim: "When canonicalizing DNS names (for both ordering
; and signing), DNS names in the RDATA section of NSEC resource records are
; not converted to lowercase. DNS names in the RDATA section of RRSIG
; resource records are converted to lowercase."
;
; hark did the exact inverse for NSEC and was correct for RRSIG, in two
; places: the parse-time RDATA scrub folded next_domain_name, and the
; canonical serializer folded it again when rebuilding the signed data. So
; any signer that preserves case in its chain failed verification and every
; NXDOMAIN and NODATA in that zone came back bogus. RFC 4034 §6.2 originally
; said to fold, RFC 3755 said not to, and 6840 settled it against 4034 to
; match what signers already do.
;
; This scenario is 003 with `C.` spelled in uppercase in the NSEC RDATA. The
; harness signs over the bytes as written, so hark and the signer only agree
; if hark also leaves them alone. Nothing else changes — the canonical
; ORDER is unaffected, since ordering compares case-insensitively and
; `a. < b. < C.` either way.
;
; The asymmetry is the point: hark signing and verifying with its own
; canonicalizer would agree with itself no matter which rule it used, so
; only an external signer can discriminate here.

RANGE_BEGIN 0 100
  ADDRESS 127.0.10.1

  ENTRY_BEGIN
    MATCH opcode qname qtype
    ADJUST copy_id copy_query
    REPLY QR AA NXDOMAIN
    SECTION QUESTION
      b. IN A
    SECTION AUTHORITY
      . 3600 IN SOA root. nobody.invalid. 1 7200 3600 86400 3600
      . 3600 IN NSEC a. SOA NS DNSKEY RRSIG NSEC
      a. 3600 IN NSEC C. A RRSIG NSEC
  ENTRY_END
RANGE_END

STEP 1 QUERY
ENTRY_BEGIN
  REPLY RD DO
  SECTION QUESTION
    b. IN A
ENTRY_END

STEP 2 CHECK_ANSWER
ENTRY_BEGIN
  MATCH flags question
  REPLY QR RD RA AD NXDOMAIN
  SECTION QUESTION
    b. IN A
ENTRY_END

SCENARIO_END
